{0 Observe}

Observe is structured logging for OCaml with a portable core and a ready
Lwt-Unix composition. The core owns admission, formatting, diagnostics,
additional drains, and deterministic capture without performing I/O.

The current package provides tagged text, deferred text, free-form values,
Repr-backed typed values, level filtering, automatic terminal formatting,
drains, diagnostics, and finite capture. It does not currently provide
wide-event or metric APIs.

{1 Installation}

Lwt-Unix applications link [(libraries observe observe.lwt-unix lwt.unix)].
Custom integrations can link the portable core with [(libraries observe)]. Add
[(preprocess (pps observe.ppx))] when using the deriver or free-form extension.

{1 Ready Lwt-Unix initialization}

{[
let config = Observe.Config.create_exn ~service:"orders" ()
let () = Observe_lwt_unix.init_exn config

let () =
  Observe.Logs.info
    (Observe.Logs.text ~tag:"startup" "service ready")
]}

The ready initializer is synchronous. It installs Lwt callback-local context,
the OS wall clock, and automatic formatted output on standard error. It starts
no scheduler and returns no logging handle. See {!module:Observe_lwt_unix}.

Readable output uses UTC [HH:mm:ss.SSS] timestamps. Tagged text stays on one
compact line. Free-form and typed structures render as ordered trees. The Unix
platform automatically selects truecolor, 256-color, or 16-color rendering of
one semantic palette for a compatible interactive standard-error terminal;
redirected output and [NO_COLOR] remain plain.

{1 Authoring}

Ordinary code logs through {!module:Observe.Logs}:

{[
Observe.Logs.info
  (Observe.Logs.text ~tag:"auth" "User logged in");

Observe.Logs.debug
  (Observe.Logs.text_lazy ~tag:"query" (fun () -> explain_query query));

Observe.Logs.info
  (Observe.Logs.free
     [%observe.value { action = "user_login"; user_id = 42 }]);

Observe.Logs.info
  (Observe.Logs.structured event_t event)
]}

Text and free-form thunks run only after level admission. Before production
initialization, logs outside an active capture are withheld and counted rather
than raising an initialization exception.

{1 Configuration and output}

{!val:Observe.Config.create} returns a validated result;
{!val:Observe.Config.create_exn} raises
{!exception:Observe.Config.Invalid_configuration}. The platform terminal is the
automatic formatted output. {!module:Observe.Drain} values are additional
application-owned outputs that receive completed {!type:Observe.Log.t} values.
Silent configuration suppresses the terminal, not drains.

{1 Runtime composition and capture}

{!module-type:Observe.Runtime.S} owns dynamic binding, cleanup, and native
cancellation. {!module-type:Observe.Platform.S} owns the wall clock, terminal
style capability, and terminal write. {!module:Observe.Runtime.Make} composes
them without adding a runtime or platform dependency to the core.

{!val:Observe.Runtime.Make.with_capture} installs capture as the innermost
dynamic route, suppresses production delivery for the callback, restores the
previous binding, and closes the capture exactly once.

For Lwt tests, {!val:Observe_lwt_unix.Test.with_capture} provides the same
bracket through real Lwt callback-local state and preserves [Lwt.Canceled].

{1 Safety boundary}

Structured values and values added with {!val:Observe.Value.embed} remain by
reference; capture is not a deep snapshot. Drains retaining asynchronous work
must synchronously copy or project what they need. Ordinary callback failures
encountered by the logging engine are contained and diagnosed, while runtime
control exceptions remain native. Arbitrary Repr descriptions do not imply
bounded traversal, cycle-safe rendering, or redaction.

Automatic Unix terminal writes are synchronous and target standard error. A
slow destination can delay the caller. Observe owns no background writer,
flush lifecycle, or durability guarantee. See the repository README for the
complete ready, test-capture, and custom-integration examples.
